    {
      "page_title": "Signals 101: What Bash Is Reacting To (cont.)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Delivery Timing"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Signal during a foreground command waits for it to finish",
            "Unless the command itself dies from that signal first",
            "Ctrl-C hits the whole foreground process group",
            "So children may already be dying when your handler runs"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "trap                  # print live traps",
            "trap -- 'cleanup' EXIT   # reusable form",
            "kill -INT \"$$\"        # scripted, not typed"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Same Signal, Two Origins",
          "items": [
            "Terminal Ctrl-C: goes to the whole group",
            "`kill -INT $$`: goes to your shell only",
            "One handler serves both, group scope differs"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Form",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`trap 'commands' SIGSPEC...` — action is a string",
            "String is re-evaluated each fire, not at definition",
            "Names work with or without `SIG`; numbers work too",
            "`trap cleanup INT`, `TERM`, `2`, `15` all valid"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "trap cleanup EXIT INT TERM HUP  # 4-net",
            "trap - INT      # restore default",
            "trap '' INT     # ignore; kids inherit",
            "trap -p EXIT    # print just this one"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Quoting Decides When"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "cards",
          "spec": {
            "cards": [
              {
                "title": "\"double\"",
                "lines": [
                  "expands NOW",
                  "freezes old value",
                  "empty if unset yet",
                  "almost never right"
                ]
              },
              {
                "title": "'single'",
                "lines": [
                  "expands at fire",
                  "sees final value",
                  "survives reassignment",
                  "the default choice"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Disarm vs Ignore"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Call",
            "Effect",
            "Children"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`trap - INT`",
              "default action",
              "default"
            ],
            [
              "`trap '' INT`",
              "ignored",
              "inherit ignore"
            ],
            [
              "`trap f INT`",
              "runs `f`",
              "reset to default"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "One Slot Per Signal",
          "text": "A second `trap ... INT` silently replaces the first. No handler chaining exists — compose everything into one function."
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Point traps at a named function, not a quoted blob",
            "Named handlers are testable and readable",
            "Save with `trap -p EXIT`, restore after a critical section"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "cont": true
    },
    {
      "page_title": "trap: Syntax, Scope, and Reset",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Where Scope Leaks"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Traps are per-shell — functions get no own scope",
            "A function's `trap` overwrites the script's handler",
            "ERR/DEBUG/RETURN skip subshells unless `set -E`/`set -T`",
            "EXIT traps also reset inside subshells"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "( trap 'echo sub' EXIT; : )  # prints sub",
            "x=$(trap 'echo x' EXIT; :)   # dies there",
            "# parent trap table untouched by both"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Scoped Cleanup Trick",
          "items": [
            "`( ... )` subshell trap fires when parens close",
            "Perfect for a temp resource used in one stage",
            "`$(...)` also forks — a trap set there is lost"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Fires On Everything",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Normal end of script, explicit `exit`, `set -e` abort",
            "Also an uncaught fatal signal that kills the shell",
            "So `trap cleanup EXIT` alone covers most cases",
            "INT/TERM traps mainly add custom messaging on top"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "What Still Slips Through"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Design For The Leak",
          "text": "`kill -9` and a hard `exec` replacing the shell both bypass EXIT entirely. Assume some runs leak and sweep at startup."
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Set the trap IMMEDIATELY after creating the resource",
            "Never before it exists — handler sees an unset variable",
            "Never several lines later — that gap is a real leak"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Status Preservation"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "cleanup(){",
            "  local rc=$?          # capture FIRST",
            "  trap - EXIT INT TERM # disarm, no recursion",
            "  rm -rf \"${tmpdir:-}\" || true",
            "  exit \"$rc\"           # keep real status",
            "}"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "trap: Syntax, Scope, and Reset (cont.)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`$?` at trap entry is the script's exit status",
            "Anything you run inside clobbers it — grab it first",
            "Script exits with that status unless the trap calls `exit N`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Handler Must Not Fail"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Idempotent and non-failing: `rm -f`, not bare `rm`",
            "Guard names: `[[ -n $tmp ]]` before touching anything",
            "Redirect noise to `/dev/null`, append `|| true`",
            "`set -e` mid-cleanup can skip the rest of teardown"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Ordering With One Slot"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "matrix",
          "spec": {
            "rows": [
              [
                "mktemp -d",
                "mkfifo"
              ],
              [
                "start child",
                "open lock"
              ]
            ],
            "caption": "cleanup stack (pop backwards)"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Only one EXIT trap, so one function tears down all",
            "Undo in reverse creation order",
            "Composable version: append to an array, iterate backwards"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Does an EXIT trap in a subshell run?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "Yes — at the closing paren, not at script end",
            "Careless `exit` inside a trap can re-enter it",
            "Disarm with `trap - EXIT` at the top of cleanup"
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "( trap 'echo sub' EXIT; work )"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Names You Must Not Pick",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`/tmp/myfile.$$` is predictable — symlink-attack vector",
            "PIDs recycle, so it collides across users too",
            "`mktemp` picks randomly and atomically instead"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "mktemp Modes"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Call",
            "Makes",
            "Perms"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`mktemp`",
              "file",
              "0600"
            ],
            [
              "`mktemp -d`",
              "directory",
              "0700"
            ],
            [
              "`-t tpl.XXXXXX`",
              "traceable name",
              "same"
            ],
            [
              "`$TMPDIR`",
              "chooses location",
              "honor it"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "One Dir Beats Many Files",
          "text": "A single `rm -rf \"$tmpdir\"` cleans everything and one trap covers all of it. Never juggle five temp paths in one handler."
        }
      ],
      "cont": true
    },
    {
      "page_title": "The EXIT Trap: Cleanup That Always Runs",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Idiom"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "tmpdir=$(mktemp -d -t iolar.XXXXXX)",
            "trap 'rm -rf -- \"$tmpdir\"' EXIT  # next line!",
            "[[ -n ${tmpdir:-} && -d $tmpdir ]] || exit 1"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Quote That Saves You"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Unquoted empty `$tmpdir` makes `rm -rf $tmpdir/` into `rm -rf /`",
            "Quote it, and use `--` to stop option parsing",
            "Guard: `[[ -n ${tmpdir:-} && -d $tmpdir ]] && rm -rf`",
            "Never build a trap path from unvalidated user input"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Why The Guard Matters",
          "items": [
            "Traps fire at the worst possible moment",
            "Half-initialised script, variable still empty",
            "`set -u` will not save you inside a trap string"
          ],
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Unlink Early, Read Later"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Deleting an open file on Linux is safe",
            "The inode survives until the last fd closes",
            "So cleanup can run while a reader still holds it"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "exec 3<>\"$f\"   # open read-write first",
            "rm -- \"$f\"     # gone from the tree now",
            "cat <&3        # still readable via fd 3"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Survives SIGKILL",
          "text": "An unlinked-but-open file vanishes even on `kill -9`, because the kernel frees the inode when the process dies. No trap needed."
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "On failure keep the dir: `[[ $rc -eq 0 ]] || echo \"kept: $tmpdir\"`",
            "`mkfifo` pipes need the same trap treatment",
            "Lock files: `flock` an fd that closes on exit"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Capture The PID Now",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`cmd &` backgrounds; `$!` holds that PID",
            "`$!` is overwritten by the very next background job",
            "`jobs -p` lists PIDs of this shell's jobs",
            "`kill $(jobs -p)` is a quick, non-recursive sweep"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Orphans And Grandchildren"
