    {
      "page_title": "Temp Files: Create, Trap, Delete",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Killing the parent does NOT kill children",
            "Orphans get reparented to init and keep running",
            "Killing a child leaves its own children alive",
            "`pkill -P $$` hits direct children only, not the tree"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Group Signaling"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Target",
            "Command",
            "Reach"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "one PID",
              "`kill $pid`",
              "that process"
            ],
            [
              "direct kids",
              "`pkill -P $$`",
              "one level"
            ],
            [
              "whole group",
              "`kill -- -$$`",
              "entire tree"
            ],
            [
              "jobs",
              "`kill $(jobs -p)`",
              "this shell"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Become Group Leader",
          "text": "`set -m` turns on job control so your script leads its own group. Without it, `kill -- -$$` can hit the caller's interactive shell."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Two-Phase Kill"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "kill -TERM \"$pid\" 2>/dev/null",
            "sleep 2                  # grace period",
            "kill -KILL \"$pid\" 2>/dev/null",
            "wait \"$pid\" 2>/dev/null  # reap it"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "TERM, wait a grace period, KILL the survivors",
            "`timeout 10 cmd` does phase one for you",
            "`timeout -k 5 10 cmd` adds the KILL 5s later",
            "Reuse `timeout` instead of hand-rolling both phases"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "wait Semantics"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Form",
            "Returns when"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`wait`",
              "all children done"
            ],
            [
              "`wait -n`",
              "any one finishes"
            ],
            [
              "`wait $pid`",
              "that child's status"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "wait Is Interruptible",
          "items": [
            "Trapped signal during `wait` runs the handler",
            "Then `wait` returns non-zero — expect it",
            "`disown -h %1` spares a job from HUP delivery"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Who Actually Gets It",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Ctrl-C sends SIGINT to the whole foreground group",
            "Your script AND every foreground child it started",
            "During a foreground child, the child usually dies first",
            "Your trap then runs after it returns — check `$?` for 130"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Un-interruptible Script"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Usability Bug",
          "text": "A script that traps INT but never exits cannot be stopped from the keyboard. Users reach for `kill -9`, and then nothing cleans up."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Killing Children: Jobs, PIDs, Groups",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Why is `exit 1` in an INT trap a lie?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "Callers cannot tell interrupt from real failure",
            "Shell wrappers and CI branch on 130 specifically",
            "Correct: clean up, then re-raise the same signal"
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "trap - INT; kill -INT \"$$\"   # true 130"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Self-Signal Idiom"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "on_int(){",
            "  cleanup",
            "  trap - INT       # restore default",
            "  kill -INT \"$$\"   # re-raise, dies at 130",
            "}"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Handler Hygiene"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Keep handlers short — signals can arrive again inside one",
            "Long loops: set a flag, do the heavy work in the loop",
            "`trap '' INT` makes a critical section uninterruptible",
            "Restore with `trap - INT` immediately after; keep it brief"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Wait style",
            "Trap latency"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`sleep 30`",
              "prompt, but blocks"
            ],
            [
              "`sleep 30 & wait $!`",
              "prompt, reapable"
            ],
            [
              "`read -t 30`",
              "interruptible"
            ],
            [
              "foreground child",
              "after it returns"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "sleep 30 & wait $!   # trap fires promptly",
            "hits=$((hits+1))",
            "(( hits > 1 )) && kill -9 -$$   # 2nd Ctrl-C"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Neighbours: HUP And TSTP"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Ctrl-C and Interrupt Handling",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Trap HUP beside INT/TERM — SSH drops are real",
            "`nohup` or `setsid` when you deliberately want survival",
            "Ctrl-Z is SIGTSTP: it stops, it does not end",
            "Never run cleanup from a TSTP trap"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Double Ctrl-C",
          "items": [
            "Count handler invocations in a variable",
            "First: graceful cleanup and message",
            "Second: skip cleanup, hard-kill the group"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "When ERR Fires",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`trap handler ERR` fires on a non-zero return",
            "Same rules that would trigger `set -e`",
            "Bash pseudo-signal, not POSIX — dash ignores it",
            "So the shebang must be `#!/usr/bin/env bash`"
